<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!-- ===========================================================================================
       Finds every architect category that calls itself storage and folds its contents into
       UnifiedStorage, whichever mod supplied it.

       This is the general net. Architect_StorageTab.xml handles Adaptive Storage Framework and LWM's
       Deep Storage by name, and this catches everything else without needing a block per mod.

       <b>Matched on defName, not on label, and that is the important decision here.</b> Label would be
       the better test of intent: a category labelled "Storage" is what a player reads as a storage tab,
       and a mod is free to label one that way with a defName like Containers.

       It cannot be used, because of what a patch operation can and cannot do. Moving the contents means
       rewriting every designationCategory whose value is the old category's defName, and there is no way
       in a single XPath to select categories by their label and then use the defNames of whatever
       matched to filter a different set of nodes. Matching the label would let this hide a category
       whose contents it had no way to move, which is strictly worse than not touching it: the buildings
       would still exist, still be filed there, and now be unreachable.

       So both halves match on the same thing, and a storage category named something else is simply
       left alone. That is a visible gap rather than a broken one.

       <b>UnifiedStorage is excluded from both halves.</b> Its own defName contains "storage", so without
       the exclusion the second operation would hide the very category this mod adds.

       The translate() call is the standard XPath trick for a case-insensitive contains: XPath 1.0 has no
       lower-case function, so the seven letters of "storage" are mapped from upper to lower before the
       test. It matches any mixture of case in those letters, which is all this needs.
       =========================================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <!-- Always, not Normal. A game with no storage mods installed matches nothing here, and that is the
         expected outcome rather than a failure worth a line in the log. -->
    <success>Always</success>
    <operations>

      <!-- Buildings. -->
      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[contains(translate(text(),'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and text()!='UnifiedStorage']</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[contains(translate(text(),'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and text()!='UnifiedStorage']/text()</xpath>
          <value>UnifiedStorage</value>
        </match>
      </li>

      <!-- Terrain, for the same reason the Space patch handles it: a mod may file a storage floor or
           platform as terrain, and leaving it behind keeps the old category populated. -->
      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/TerrainDef/designationCategory[contains(translate(text(),'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and text()!='UnifiedStorage']</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/TerrainDef/designationCategory[contains(translate(text(),'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and text()!='UnifiedStorage']/text()</xpath>
          <value>UnifiedStorage</value>
        </match>
      </li>

      <!-- Then hide what has just been emptied.

           not(hidden) keeps this from adding a second hidden element to a category one of the targeted
           patches has already marked. The field's reader tolerates duplicates and takes the last, so
           this is not load bearing, but a def carrying the same element twice is untidy and would
           mislead anyone reading the patched XML. -->
      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[contains(translate(defName,'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and defName!='UnifiedStorage' and not(hidden)]</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[contains(translate(defName,'STORAGE','storage'),'storage') and defName!='UnifiedStorage' and not(hidden)]</xpath>
          <value>
            <hidden>true</hidden>
          </value>
        </match>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
